# DESCRIPTION

The functions are C23's, on `double` and, with an `f` suffix, `float`.

## Exact functions

These have one right answer and give it, bit for bit as glibc does.
`floor`, `ceil` and `trunc` round to an integer down, up and toward zero.
`round` rounds to nearest with ties away from zero, and `roundeven` with ties to even.
`rint` and `nearbyint` round to nearest, ties to even: there is no `<fenv.h>`, the rounding mode never changes, and neither raises the inexact exception.
`fabs` and `copysign` act on the sign bit.
`sqrt` is the processor's instruction, correctly rounded.
`fmod` and `remainder` are exact; the quotient is rounded toward zero for `fmod` and to nearest, ties to even, for `remainder`.
`fma` computes x times y plus z rounded once; `fmaf` rounds once to `float`.
`ldexp` and `scalbn` (the radix is 2, so they are the same function) return x times 2 to the <n>, rounded once.
`frexp` returns a significand in [0.5, 1) and stores the exponent in <e>; for zero, infinity and NaN it returns x and stores 0.
`modf` returns the fractional part of x and stores the integer part in <i>, both with x's sign.
`fmodf` and `remainderf` are exact too.

## Transcendental functions

`exp`, `exp2`, `log`, `log2`, `log10`, `sin` and `cos` are within one unit in the last place of the true value, measured against glibc; they are not always correctly rounded.
The `float` versions (`expf`, `sinf` and the rest) compute the `double` result and round it, so they can differ from the correctly rounded `float` in rare cases.
`exp` of a large argument is infinity, and of a very negative one zero.
`log` of a negative number is NaN and of zero is negative infinity.
`sin` and `cos` of an infinity are NaN.
Large arguments to `sin` and `cos` are reduced exactly.

## Header

The header defines `HUGE_VAL`, `HUGE_VALF`, `INFINITY`, `NAN`, `M_PI`, `M_E`, `FP_NAN`, `FP_INFINITE`, `FP_ZERO`, `FP_SUBNORMAL`, `FP_NORMAL`, the types `float_t` and `double_t`, and the macros `isnan`, `isinf`, `isfinite`, `signbit`, `fpclassify`, `isnormal`, `isgreater`, `isgreaterequal`, `isless`, `islessequal`, `islessgreater` and `isunordered`, which are the compiler's builtins.

